 of the map terrain. With "(auto)" a good value will be picked after terrain generationÓÄ∑(auto)\You can't set the map height limit to this value. At least one mountain on the map is higher4Allow landscaping under buildings, tracks, etc.: ÓÄÆBAllow landscaping under buildings and tracks without removing them3Allow more realistically sized catchment areas: ÓÄÆSHave differently sized catchment areas for different types of stations and airportsICompany stations can serve industries with attached neutral stations: ÓÄÆ¡OWhen enabled, industries with attached stations (such as Oil Rigs) may also be served by company-owned stations built nearby. When disabled, these industries may only be served by their attached stations. Any nearby company stations won't be able to serve them, nor will the attached station serve anything else other than the industry@Allow removal of more town-owned roads, bridges and tunnels: ÓÄÆ@Make it easier to remove town-owned infrastructure and buildingsMaximum length of trains: ÓÄÆ Set the maximum length of trainsÓÄµ tileÓÄº␀␀␀s#Amount of vehicle smoke/sparks: ÓÄÆ=Set how much smoke or how many sparks are emitted by vehiclesTrain acceleration model: ÓÄÆ¿ÚSelect the physics model for train acceleration. The "original" model penalises slopes equally for all vehicles. The "realistic" model penalises slopes and curves depending on various properties of the consist, like length and tractive effort$Road vehicle acceleration model: ÓÄÆ¿ÎSelect the physics model for road vehicle acceleration. The "original" model penalises slopes equally for all vehicles. The "realistic" model penalises slopes depending on various properties of the engine, for example 'tractive effort'Slope steepness for trains: ÓÄÆ\Steepness of a sloped tile for a train. Higher values make it more difficult to climb a hillÓÄµ%&Slope steepness for road vehicles: ÓÄÆcSteepness of a sloped tile for a road vehicle. Higher values make it more difficult to climb a hill)Forbid trains from making 90¬∞ turns: ÓÄÆ¿ı90 degree turns occur when a horizontal track is directly followed by a vertical track piece on the adjacent tile, thus making the train turn by 90 degree when traversing the tile edge instead of the usual 45 degrees for other track combinations1Allow to join stations not directly adjacent: ÓÄÆzAllow adding parts to a station without directly touching the existing parts, by Ctrl+Clicking while placing the new partsInflation: ÓÄÆUEnable inflation in the economy, where costs are slightly faster rising than paymentsMaximum bridge length: ÓÄÆ#Maximum length for building bridgesMaximum bridge height: ÓÄÆ#Maximum height for building bridgesMaximum tunnel length: ÓÄÆ#Maximum length for building tunnels0Manual primary industry construction method: ÓÄÆ¡EMethod of funding a primary industry. 'none' means it is not possible to fund any, 'prospecting' means funding is possible, but construction occurs in a random spot on the map and may as well fail, 'as other industries' means raw industries can be constructed by companies like processing industries in any position they likeNoneAs other industriesProspecting Flat area around industries: ÓÄÆâAmount of flat space around an industry. This ensures empty space will remain available around an industry for building tracks, et cetera/Allow multiple similar industries per town: ÓÄÆôNormally, a town does not want more than one industry of each type. With this setting, it will allow several industries of the same type in the same townShow signals: ÓÄÆ2Select on which side of the track to place signalsOn the leftOn the driving sideOn the right0Show finances window at the end of the year: ÓÄÆ2Show finances window at the end of the period: ÓÄÆIf enabled, the finances window pops up at the end of each year to allow easy inspection of the financial status of the companyÅIf enabled, the finances window pops up at the end of each period to allow easy inspection of the financial status of the company)New orders are 'non-stop' by default: ÓÄÆ¡9Normally, a vehicle will stop at every station it passes. By enabling this setting, it will drive through all station on the way to its final destination without stopping. Note, that this setting only defines a default value for new orders. Individual orders can be set explicitly to either behaviour nevertheless;New train orders stop by default at the ÓÄÆ of the platform¡_Place where a train will stop at the platform by default. The 'near end' means close to the entry point, 'middle' means in the middle of the platform, and 'far end' means far away from the entry point. Note, that this setting only defines a default value for new orders. Individual orders can have their stop location set by clicking on the order textnear endmiddlefar end)Pan window when mouse is at the edge: ÓÄÆZWhen enabled, viewports will start to scroll when the mouse is near the edge of the windowDisabledMain viewport, full-screen onlyMain viewportEvery viewport)Allow bribing of the local authority: ÓÄÆ†Allow companies to try bribing the local town authority. If the bribe is noticed by an inspector, the company will not be able to act in the town for six months°Allow companies to try bribing the local town authority. If the bribe is noticed by an inspector, the company will not be able to act in the town for six minutes,Allow buying exclusive transport rights: ÓÄÆåIf a company buys exclusive transport rights for a town, opponents' stations (passenger and cargo) won't receive any cargo for twelve monthsçIf a company buys exclusive transport rights for a town, opponents' stations (passenger and cargo) won't receive any cargo for twelve minutesAllow funding buildings: ÓÄÆ=Allow companies to give money to towns for funding new houses,Allow funding local road reconstruction: ÓÄÆkAllow companies to give money to towns for road re-construction to sabotage road-based services in the town+Allow sending money to other companies: ÓÄÆ=Allow transfer of money between companies in multiplayer mode;Weight multiplier for freight to simulate heavy trains: ÓÄÆÇSet the impact of carrying freight in trains. A higher value makes carrying freight more demanding for trains, especially at hillsPlane speed factor: ÓÄÆySet the relative speed of planes compared to other vehicle types, to reduce the amount of income of transport by aircraft1 / ÓÄµNumber of plane crashes: ÓÄÆÑSet the chance of a random aircraft crash happening.
* Large airplanes always have a risk of crashing when landing on small airportsNone*ReducedNormalCAllow level crossings with roads or rails owned by competitors: ÓÄÆLAllow construction of level crossings on roads or rails owned by competitors;Allow drive-through road stops on roads owned by towns: ÓÄÆFAllow construction of drive-through road stops on roads owned by townsAAllow drive-through road stops on roads owned by competitors: ÓÄÆPAllow construction of drive-through road stops on roads owned by other companies=Changing this setting is not possible when there are vehiclesInfrastructure maintenance: ÓÄÆ•When enabled, infrastructure causes maintenance costs. The cost grows over-proportional with the network size, thus affecting bigger companies more than smaller onesStarting company colour: ÓÄÆ&Choose starting colour for the company&Starting company secondary colour: ÓÄÆSChoose starting secondary colour for the company, if using a NewGRF that enables itAirports never expire: ÓÄÆ[Enabling this setting makes each airport type stay available forever after its introductionWarn if vehicle is lost: ÓÄÆRTrigger messages about vehicles unable to find a path to their ordered destinationReview vehicles' orders: ÓÄÆâWhen enabled, the orders of the vehicles are periodically checked, and some obvious issues are reported with a news message when detectedNo!Yes, but exclude stopped vehiclesOf all vehicles+Warn if a vehicle's income is negative: ÓÄÆ[When enabled, a news message gets sent when a vehicle has not made any profit within a year]When enabled, a news message gets sent when a vehicle has not made any profit within a period%Warn if a vehicle is getting old: ÓÄÆDWhen enabled, a news message gets sent when a vehicle is getting oldVehicles never expire: ÓÄÆRWhen enabled, all vehicle models remain available forever after their introductionTimekeeping: ÓÄÆ¬LSelect the timekeeping units of the game. This cannot be changed later.

Calendar-based is the classic OpenTTD experience, with a year consisting of 12 months, and each month having 28-31 days.

In Wallclock-based time, cargo production and financials are instead based on one-minute increments, which is about as long as a 30 day month takes in Calendar-based mode. These are grouped into 12-minute periods, equivalent to a year in Calendar-based mode.

In either mode there is always a classic calendar, which is used for introduction dates of vehicles, houses, and other infrastructureCalendar	WallclockMinutes per year: ÓÄÆ¡~Choose the number of minutes in a calendar year. The default is 12 minutes. Set to 0 to stop calendar time from changing. Increasing the length of the calendar year slows down the introduction of vehicles, houses, and other infrastructure. It does not affect vehicle speed or economic simulation, except for inflation. This setting is only available when using wallclock timekeepingÓÄ∑0 (calendar time frozen) Scale town cargo production: ÓÄÆ6Scale the cargo production of towns by this percentage$Scale industry cargo production: ÓÄÆ;Scale the cargo production of industries by this percentageÓÄ∑%#Scale cargo payment aging rate: ÓÄÆIAdjust how quickly cargo loaded on a vehicle decreases in value over timeÓÄ∑%No decay'Autorenew vehicle when it gets old: ÓÄÆsWhen enabled, a vehicle nearing its end of life gets automatically replaced when the renew conditions are fulfilled)Autorenew when vehicle is ÓÄÆ maximum ageBRelative age when a vehicle should be considered for auto-renewingÓÄµ monthÓÄº␀␀␀s beforeÓÄµ monthÓÄº␀␀␀s after-Autorenew minimum needed money for renew: ÓÄÆ^Minimal amount of money that must remain in the bank before considering auto-renewing vehiclesDuration of error message: ÓÄÆ™Duration for displaying error messages in a red window. Note that some (critical) error messages are not closed automatically after this time, but must be closed manuallyShow tooltips: ÓÄÆØDelay before tooltips are displayed when hovering the mouse over some interface element. Alternatively tooltips are bound to the right mouse button when this value is set to 0"Hover for ÓÄµ millisecondÓÄº␀␀␀sRight click0Show town population in the town name label: ÓÄÆ9Display the population of towns in their label on the map!Thickness of lines in graphs: ÓÄÆéWidth of the line in the graphs. A thin line is more precisely readable, a thicker line is easier to see and colours are easier to distinguish7Show the NewGRF's name in the build vehicle window: ÓÄÆ\Add a line to the build vehicle window, showing which NewGRF the selected vehicle comes from@Show the cargoes the vehicles can carry in the list windows: ÓÄÆVIf enabled, the vehicle's transportable load will appear above it in the vehicle listsLandscape: ÓÄÆíLandscapes define basic gameplay scenarios with different cargoes and town growth requirements. NewGRF and Game Scripts allow finer control thoughLand generator: ÓÄÆ®The original generator depends on the base graphics set, and composes fixed landscape shapes. TerraGenesis is a Perlin noise based generator with finer control settingsOriginalTerraGenesisMaximum height preset: ÓÄÆ9Choose the height of hills and mountains of the landscapeIndustry density: ÓÄÆ_Set how many industries should be generated and what level should be maintained during the game2Maximum distance from edge for Oil industries: ÓÄÆ¿¡Limit for how far from the map border oil refineries and oil rigs can be constructed. On island maps this ensures they are near the coast. On maps larger than 256 tiles, this value is scaled upSnow line height: ÓÄÆ¿÷Choose at what height snow starts in sub-arctic landscape. Snow also affects industry generation and town growth requirements. Can only be modified via Scenario Editor or is otherwise calculated via "snow coverage"Snow coverage: ÓÄÆ¿—Choose the approximate amount of snow on the sub-arctic landscape. Snow also affects industry generation and town growth requirements. Only used during map generation. Sea level and coast tiles never have snowÓÄ∑%Desert coverage: ÓÄÆ®Choose the approximate amount of desert on the tropical landscape. Desert also affects industry generation and town growth requirements. Only used during map generationÓÄ∑%Roughness of terrain: ÓÄÆÄChoose the shape and number of hills. Smooth landscapes have fewer, wider hills, while rough landscapes have more, smaller hillsVery SmoothSmoothRough
