No actionsAll non-construction actions#All but landscape modifying actionsAll actionsUse groups in vehicle list: ÓÄÆ@Enable usage of the advanced vehicle lists for grouping vehiclesUse loading indicators: ÓÄÆSSelect whether loading indicators are displayed above loading or unloading vehiclesTime units for timetables: ÓÄÆ1Select the time units used for vehicle timetablesDaysSecondsTicks-Show arrival and departure in timetables: ÓÄÆ=Display anticipated arrival and departure times in timetables%Quick creation of vehicle orders: ÓÄÆ;Pre-select the 'goto cursor' when opening the orders windowADefault rail, road, and tram type (after new game/game load): ÓÄÆ¿ÓRail, road, and tram type to select after starting or loading a game. 'first available' selects the oldest type of tracks, 'last available' selects the newest type of tracks, and 'most used' selects the type which is currently most in useFirst availableLast available	Most used&Show path reservations for tracks: ÓÄÆmGive reserved tracks a different colour to assist in problems with trains refusing to enter path-based blocks+Keep building tools active after usage: ÓÄÆAKeep the building tools for bridges, tunnels, etc. open after use:Automatically remove signals during rail construction: ÓÄÆâAutomatically remove signals during rail construction if the signals are in the way. Note that this can potentially lead to train crashesFast forward speed limit: ÓÄÆ¿ÙLimit on how fast the game goes when fast forward is enabled. 0 = no limit (as fast as your computer allows). Values below 100% slow the game down. The upper-limit depends on the specification of your computer and can vary depending on the gameÓÄ∑% normal game speed*No limit (as fast as your computer allows)News ticker: ÓÄÆ'Play sound for summarised news messagesNewspaper: ÓÄÆ%Play sound upon display of newspapersEnd of year: ÓÄÆEnd of period: ÓÄÆsPlay sound at the end of a year summarising the company's performance during the year compared to the previous yearyPlay sound at the end of a period summarising the company's performance during the period compared to the previous periodConstruction: ÓÄÆ7Play sound on successful constructions or other actionsButton clicks: ÓÄÆBeep when clicking buttonsDisasters/accidents: ÓÄÆ-Play sound effects of accidents and disastersVehicles: ÓÄÆPlay sound effects of vehiclesAmbient: ÓÄÆ6Play ambient sounds of landscape, industries and towns)Maximum number of trains per company: ÓÄÆ0Maximum number of trains that a company can have0Maximum number of road vehicles per company: ÓÄÆ7Maximum number of road vehicles that a company can have+Maximum number of aircraft per company: ÓÄÆ2Maximum number of aircraft that a company can have(Maximum number of ships per company: ÓÄÆ/Maximum number of ships that a company can have Disable trains for computer: ÓÄÆLEnabling this setting makes building trains impossible for a computer player'Disable road vehicles for computer: ÓÄÆSEnabling this setting makes building road vehicles impossible for a computer player"Disable aircraft for computer: ÓÄÆNEnabling this setting makes building aircraft impossible for a computer playerDisable ships for computer: ÓÄÆKEnabling this setting makes building ships impossible for a computer playerAllow AIs in multiplayer: ÓÄÆ=Allow AI computer players to participate in multiplayer games*#opcodes before scripts are suspended: ÓÄÆFMaximum number of computation steps that a script can take in one turn Max memory usage per script: ÓÄÆyHow much memory a single script may consume before it's forcibly terminated. This may need to be increased for large mapsÓÄµ MiB&Service intervals are in percents: ÓÄÆ¡When enabled, vehicles try to service when their reliability drops by a given percentage of the maximum reliability.

For example, if a vehicle's maximum reliability is 90% and the service interval is 20%, the vehicle will try to service when it reaches 72% reliability(Default service interval for trains: ÓÄÆnSet the default service interval for new rail vehicles, if no explicit service interval is set for the vehicle/Default service interval for road vehicles: ÓÄÆnSet the default service interval for new road vehicles, if no explicit service interval is set for the vehicle*Default service interval for aircraft: ÓÄÆiSet the default service interval for new aircraft, if no explicit service interval is set for the vehicle'Default service interval for ships: ÓÄÆfSet the default service interval for new ships, if no explicit service interval is set for the vehicleÓÄµ¬†DayÓÄº␀␀␀sÓÄµ¬†MinuteÓÄº␀␀␀sÓÄµ¬†%Disabled2Disable servicing when breakdowns set to none: ÓÄÆDWhen enabled, vehicles do not get serviced if they cannot break downFLoading speed penalty for trains that are longer than the station: ÓÄÆñWhen enabled, trains which are too long for the station load more slowly than a train which fits the station. This setting does not affect pathfindingEnable wagon speed limits: ÓÄÆWWhen enabled, also use speed limits of wagons for deciding the maximum speed of a train"Enable aircraft maximum range: ÓÄÆEWhen enabled, NewGRFs will be able to limit maximum range of aircraftDisable electric rails: ÓÄÆgEnabling this setting disables the requirement to electrify tracks to make electric engines run on them1Arrival of first vehicle at player's station: ÓÄÆLDisplay a newspaper when the first vehicle arrives at a new player's station5Arrival of first vehicle at competitor's station: ÓÄÆPDisplay a newspaper when the first vehicle arrives at a new competitor's stationAccidents / disasters: ÓÄÆ5Display a newspaper when accidents or disasters occur'Accidents of competitor's vehicles: ÓÄÆ:Display a newspaper about crashed vehicles for competitorsCompany information: ÓÄÆXDisplay a newspaper when a new company starts, or when companies are risking to bankruptOpening of industries: ÓÄÆ,Display a newspaper when new industries openClosing of industries: ÓÄÆ.Display a newspaper when industries close downEconomy changes: ÓÄÆ3Display a newspaper about global changes to economy;Production changes of industries served by the company: ÓÄÆcDisplay a newspaper when the production level of industries change, which are served by the company=Production changes of industries served by competitor(s): ÓÄÆgDisplay a newspaper when the production level of industries change, which are served by the competitors&Other industry production changes: ÓÄÆvDisplay a newspaper when the production level of industries change, which are not served by the company or competitors/Advice / information on company's vehicles: ÓÄÆ1Display messages about vehicles needing attentionNew vehicles: ÓÄÆ=Display a newspaper when a new vehicle type becomes available Changes to cargo acceptance: ÓÄÆCDisplay messages about stations changing acceptance of some cargoesSubsidies: ÓÄÆ0Display a newspaper about subsidy related eventsGeneral information: ÓÄÆnDisplay newspaper about general events, such as purchase of exclusive rights or funding of road reconstructionOffSummaryFullColoured news appears in: ÓÄÆmYear that the newspaper announcements get printed in colour. Before this year, it uses monochrome black/whiteStarting year: ÓÄÆScoring end year: ÓÄÆ¡Year the game ends for scoring purposes. At the end of this year, the company's score is recorded and the high-score screen is displayed, but the players can continue playing after that.
If this is before the starting year, the high-score screen is never displayedÓÄ∑NeverEconomy type: ÓÄÆ¡ÉSmooth economy makes production changes more often, and in smaller steps. Frozen economy stops production changes and industry closures. This setting may have no effect if industry types are provided by a NewGRF.

Industry production per month may still appear to vary if the economy is Frozen, because production is triggered at fixed intervals which do not exactly correspond to months¡ÖSmooth economy makes production changes more often, and in smaller steps. Frozen economy stops production changes and industry closures. This setting may have no effect if industry types are provided by a NewGRF.

Industry production per minute may still appear to vary if the economy is Frozen, because production is triggered at fixed intervals which do not exactly correspond to minutesOriginalSmoothFrozen6Percentage of leg profit to pay in feeder systems: ÓÄÆjPercentage of income given to the intermediate legs in feeder systems, giving more control over the income'When dragging, place signals every: ÓÄÆ}Set the distance at which signals will be built on a track up to the next obstacle (signal, junction), if signals are draggedÓÄµ tileÓÄº␀␀␀s7When dragging, keep fixed distance between signals: ÓÄÆ¡Select the behaviour of signal placement when Ctrl+dragging signals. If disabled, signals are placed around tunnels or bridges to avoid long stretches without signals. If enabled, signals are placed every n tiles, making alignment of signals at parallel tracks easier*Automatically build semaphores before: ÓÄÆ©Set the year when electric signals will be used for tracks. Before this year, non-electric signals will be used (which have the exact same function, but different looks)Cycle through signal types: ÓÄÆdSelect which signal types to cycle through when Ctrl+Clicking on a built signal with the signal toolCurrent group onlyAll visibleShow signal types: ÓÄÆ9Choose which signal types are shown in the signal toolbarPath signals onlyAll signalsRoad layout for new towns: ÓÄÆ$Layout for the road network of townsOriginalBetter roads2x2 grid3x3 gridRandom%Towns are allowed to build roads: ÓÄÆiAllow towns to build roads for growth. Disable to prevent town authorities from building roads themselves/Towns are allowed to build level crossings: ÓÄÆ;Enabling this setting allows towns to build level crossings1Limit airport placement based on noise level: ÓÄÆ¡Allow towns to block airport construction based on their noise acceptance level, which is based on the town's population and airport size and distance. If this setting is disabled, towns allow only two airports unless the local authority attitude is set to "Permissive"Founding towns in game: ÓÄÆCEnabling this setting allows players to found new towns in the game	ForbiddenAllowedAllowed, custom town layout#Placing individual town houses: ÓÄÆBEnabling this setting allows players to place town houses manually	ForbiddenAllowedAllowed, fully constructedTown cargo generation: ÓÄÆ¿˝How much cargo is produced by houses in towns, relative to the overall population of the town.
Quadratic growth: A town twice the size generates four times as many passengers.
Linear growth: A town twice the size generates twice the amount of passengersQuadratic (original)LinearIn game placement of trees: ÓÄÆÑControl random appearance of trees during the game. This might affect industries which rely on tree growth, for example lumber mills-Grow but don't spread ÓÅÖ(breaks lumber mill)#Grow but only spread in rainforestsGrow and spread everywhere0Don't grow, don't spread ÓÅÖ(breaks lumber mill)Position of main toolbar: ÓÄÆ@Horizontal position of the main toolbar at the top of the screenPosition of status bar: ÓÄÆAHorizontal position of the status bar at the bottom of the screenWindow snap radius: ÓÄÆaDistance between windows before the window being moved is automatically aligned to nearby windowsÓÄµ pixelÓÄº␀␀␀sDisabled)Maximum number of non-sticky windows: ÓÄÆjNumber of non-sticky open windows before old windows get automatically closed to make room for new windowsÓÄµDisabledMaximum zoom in level: ÓÄÆoThe maximum zoom-in level for viewports. Note that enabling higher zoom-in levels increases memory requirementsMaximum zoom out level: ÓÄÆZThe maximum zoom-out level for viewports. Higher zoom-out levels might cause lag when used4x2xNormal2x4x8x&Highest resolution sprites to use: ÓÄÆ¡Limit the maximum resolution to use for sprites. Limiting sprite resolution will avoid using high resolution graphics even when available. This can help keep the game appearance unified when using a mix of NewGRF files with and without high resolution graphics4x2x1xTown growth speed: ÓÄÆSpeed of town growthNoneSlowNormalFast	Very fast0Proportion of towns that will become cities: ÓÄÆ^Amount of towns which will become a city, thus a town which starts out larger and grows faster1 in ÓÄµNone!Initial city size multiplier: ÓÄÆDAverage size of cities relative to normal towns at start of the game#Minimum distance between towns: ÓÄÆVSet the minimum distance in tiles between towns for map generation and random founding#Update distribution graph every ÓÄÆ¡±Time between subsequent recalculations of the link graph. Each recalculation calculates the plans for one component of the graph. That means that a value X for this setting does not mean the whole graph will be updated every X seconds. Only some component will. The shorter you set it the more CPU time will be necessary to calculate it. The longer you set it the longer it will take until the cargo distribution starts on new routes0Take ÓÄÆ for recalculation of distribution graph¡îTime taken for each recalculation of a link graph component. When a recalculation is started, a thread is spawned which is allowed to run for this number of seconds. The shorter you set this the more likely it is that the thread is not finished when it's supposed to. Then the game stops until it is ("lag"). The longer you set it the longer it takes for the distribution to be updated when routes change%Distribution mode for passengers: ÓÄÆ¡"Symmetric" means that roughly the same number of passengers will go from a station A to a station B as from B to A. "Asymmetric" means that arbitrary numbers of passengers can go in either direction. "Manual" means that no automatic distribution will take place for passengersDistribution mode for mail: ÓÄÆ¡"Symmetric" means that roughly the same amount of mail will be sent from a station A to a station B as from B to A. "Asymmetric" means that arbitrary amounts of mail can be sent in either direction. "Manual" means that no automatic distribution will take place for mail3Distribution mode for the ARMOURED cargo class: ÓÄÆ¬°The ARMOURED cargo class contains valuables in the temperate, diamonds in the subtropical, or gold in the subarctic climate. NewGRFs may change that. "Symmetric" means that roughly the same amount of that cargo will be sent from a station A to a station B as from B to A. "Asymmetric" means that arbitrary amounts of that cargo can be sent in either direction. "Manual" means that no automatic distribution will take place for that cargo. It is recommended to set this to asymmetric or manual when playing subarctic or subtropic, as banks only receive cargo in these climates. For temperate you can also choose symmetric as banks will send valuables back to the origin bank.Distribution mode for other cargo classes: ÓÄÆ£"Asymmetric" means that arbitrary amounts of cargo can be sent in either direction. "Manual" means that no automatic distribution will take place for those cargoesManual
Asymmetric	SymmetricDistribution accuracy: ÓÄÆ¡The higher you set this the more CPU time the calculation of the link graph will take. If it takes too long you may notice lag. If you set it to a low value, however, the distribution will be inaccurate, and you may notice cargo not being sent to the places you expect it to go"Effect of distance on demands: ÓÄÆ¡nIf you set this to a value higher than 0, the distance between the origin station A of some cargo and a possible destination B will have an effect on the amount of cargo sent from A to B. The further away B is from A the less cargo will be sent. The higher you set it, the less cargo will be sent to far away stations and the more cargo will be sent to near stations1Amount of returning cargo for symmetric mode: ÓÄÆ¡Setting this to less than 100% makes the symmetric distribution behave more like the asymmetric one. Less cargo will be forcibly sent back if a certain amount is sent to a station. If you set it to 0% the symmetric distribution behaves just like the asymmetric one?Saturation of short paths before using high-capacity paths: ÓÄÆ¬æFrequently there are multiple paths between two given stations. Cargodist will saturate the shortest path first, then use the second shortest path until that is saturated and so on. Saturation is determined by an estimation of capacity and planned usage. Once it has saturated all paths, if there is still demand left, it will overload all paths, prefering the ones with high capacity. Most of the time the algorithm will not estimate the capacity accurately, though. This setting allows you to specify up to which percentage a shorter path must be saturated in the first pass before choosing the next longer one. Set it to less than 100% to avoid overcrowded stations in case of overestimated capacitySpeed units (land): ÓÄÆSpeed units (nautical): ÓÄÆNWhenever a speed is shown in the user interface, show it in the selected unitsImperial (mph)Metric (km/h)SI (m/s)Game units (tiles/day)Game units (tiles/sec)KnotsVehicle power units: ÓÄÆXWhenever a vehicle's power is shown in the user interface, show it in the selected unitsImperial (hp)Metric (hp)SI (kW)Weights units: ÓÄÆOWhenever a weight is shown in the user interface, show it in the selected unitsImperial (short t/ton)Metric (t/tonne)SI (kg)Volumes units: ÓÄÆOWhenever a volume is shown in the user interface, show it in the selected unitsImperial (gal)
Metric (l)SI (m¬≥)Tractive effort units: ÓÄÆwWhenever a tractive effort (also known as tractive force) is shown in the user interface, show it in the selected unitsImperial (lbf)Metric (kgf)SI (kN)Heights units: ÓÄÆOWhenever a height is shown in the user interface, show it in the selected unitsImperial (ft)
